// Signing.xcconfig — the one place the code-signing settings live; project.yml includes it for both
// build configurations. The Team ID reaches the code only through the `TeamID` Info.plist key (the
// app's Info.plist and the helper's in-binary Info.plist section); no .swift file hard-codes one.
//
// SET YOUR TEAM ID before you build: write it after `DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =` below, in this file. The
// install and purge scripts read it from here, so passing it to xcodebuild alone is not enough.
// It is the 10-character Organizational Unit
// (OU) of your signing certificate, NOT the code in parentheses that
// `security find-identity -v -p codesigning` prints after your name. Xcode > Settings > Accounts
// shows it, or read the OU= field of:
//   security find-certificate -c "Apple Development" -p | openssl x509 -noout -subject
// A free Personal Team (any Apple ID added to Xcode) is enough to build and run the app.
// Left empty, the build fails with Xcode's own "requires a development team" error.
// With no Apple account at all, `xcodebuild ... CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=-` builds and tests ad hoc, but
// that app stops at launch ("TeamID missing from Info.plist"): the privileged helper's code-signing
// pins are built from the Team ID and refuse to run without one.
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =

// Manual code signing on all targets.
CODE_SIGN_STYLE = Manual

// "Apple Development" is the identity a free Personal Team provides, and it is enough for the
// privileged helper's local SMAppService registration. Switch this to "Developer ID Application"
// (paid Apple Developer Program) for a build you hand to someone else.
CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = Apple Development
